# Every IZ4 carries the foundation word for word: human intention protected.
# It begins with Invariant Zero, humans first: https://iz4.you/foundation. It is the same in every IZ4 and not yours to edit.
# Your own invariants are named under your project's domain.

IS FOR WHAT?  jmp is a terminal tool for getting from "I found something" to "I
              am editing it". It takes search results, the output of any
              command, or the places you jumped to recently, lets you pick a
              location and inspect it in a preview, and opens your editor at
              that file and line without leaving the terminal. jmp is glue: it
              finds and presents locations and hands the chosen one to the tools
              you already use.

IS FOR WHO?   Developers working in the terminal who want to get from a search
              result to editing it quickly.


editor-is-the-destination:
  WHERE:    a jump ends by opening the configured editor on a file
  ALWAYS:   The editor is the destination. Every jmp ends by opening the human's
            configured editor at a specific file and line. jmp itself never
            modifies a file, and it never invokes the editor for a file that
            does not exist.
  PROTECTS: jmp is glue between finding something and editing it. The human's
            own editor does the editing, and jmp earns trust by never touching a
            file itself and never sending the editor somewhere that does not
            exist.

tools-are-your-own:
  WHERE:    jmp needs an editor, search, file locating or browser command, or a
            first run creates the config
  ALWAYS:   The tools are the human's own. The editor, in-file search, file
            locating and browser commands are command templates read from the
            config file at `~/.jmp`; jmp orchestrates them and hard-codes none
            of them. On first run the config file is created with working
            defaults and commented cross-platform alternatives.
  PROTECTS: Jmp works out of the box and the config file teaches its own format.

every-jump-remembered:
  WHERE:    a jump completes or bare jmp is run
  ALWAYS:   Every jmp is remembered. Completing a jmp records both the command
            that led there and the chosen destination - path, line and matching
            text - in `~/.jmp.hist`, most recent first, capped at the last 500
            entries. Running bare `jmp` replays that history.
  PROTECTS: Places you have already been should be one keypress away; the
            history is what makes bare jmp useful.

jumps-from-any-output:
  WHERE:    jmp on runs a command and its output is searched for file references
  ALWAYS:   `jmp on` accepts any command. It runs the command as given, captures
            both standard output and standard error, and lets the human jump to
            any file reference found in that combined output. Arbitrary output
            never has to parse cleanly for jmp to be useful on it.
  PROTECTS: Useful file references turn up in the output of every tool,
            including error output, and jmp should work on them without needing
            a parser for each tool.

lands-on-exact-line:
  WHERE:    in-file search hits are presented or one is chosen to jump to
  ALWAYS:   Search is line-addressed. In-file search presents hits grouped by
            file with the matching line and its line number, and jumping to a
            hit opens the editor at that exact line, not merely at the file.
  PROTECTS: Getting to the exact line is the whole point; landing at the top of
            the file leaves the human searching again.

